# EX200 Podman & Containers – Conceptual Notes

> These notes summarize the key concepts behind Podman, images, containers, rootless containers, volume mounts, and systemd services based on hands-on practice.

---

# 1. Everything revolves around the Image

Initially, it seems like there are many separate concepts:

* Containerfile
* Image
* Container
* Volume Mounts
* Systemd
* Rootless Containers
* Auto-start

In reality, they all follow one workflow.

```text
Containerfile
      │
      ▼
podman build
      │
      ▼
Image
      │
      ▼
podman run
      │
      ▼
Container
      │
      ▼
podman generate systemd
      │
      ▼
systemctl --user enable
      │
      ▼
Automatic startup after reboot
```

Everything begins with an **Image**.

---

# 2. Image = Blueprint (Template)

An image is **not** a running container.

Think of it as a template.

```text
Image
   │
   ├────► Container 1
   ├────► Container 2
   ├────► Container 3
   └────► Container 4
```

One image can create **unlimited containers**.

Example:

```bash
podman run --name web1 meraimage
podman run --name web2 meraimage
podman run --name web3 meraimage
```

The image remains unchanged.

---

# 3. Containerfile ≠ Container

The Containerfile contains instructions.

Example:

```dockerfile
FROM registry.access.redhat.com/ubi9/ubi:latest

CMD ["sleep", "infinity"]
```

Workflow:

```text
Containerfile
      │
      ▼
Build
      │
      ▼
Image
      │
      ▼
Run
      │
      ▼
Container
```

---

# 4. Understanding "."

Command:

```bash
podman build -t myimage .
```

Many beginners think "." means:

> Use this file.

It actually means:

> Use this directory as the build context.

Podman automatically searches only for:

```
Containerfile
```

or

```
Dockerfile
```

If your file has another name:

```
mycontainerfile
```

then use:

```bash
podman build -t myimage -f mycontainerfile .
```

---

# 5. Host vs Container

Volume mount syntax:

```bash
-v HOST_PATH:CONTAINER_PATH:OPTIONS
```

Always remember:

```text
HOST
 │
 ▼
CONTAINER
```

Example:

```bash
-v /opt/files:/opt/incoming:Z
```

means

```text
Host
/opt/files

↓

Container
/opt/incoming
```

Left side = Host

Right side = Container

---

# 6. Why ":Z"?

```bash
-v /opt/files:/opt/incoming:Z
```

The `:Z` tells SELinux to relabel the mounted directory so the container can access it.

Without `:Z`, you may receive:

```
Permission denied
```

even when Linux file permissions are correct.

---

# 7. Does Podman create Container Directories?

Suppose your image contains only:

```dockerfile
FROM registry.access.redhat.com/ubi9/ubi:latest

CMD ["sleep","infinity"]
```

There is no:

```
/opt/incoming
```

or

```
/opt/outgoing
```

When you run:

```bash
podman run \
-v /opt/files:/opt/incoming \
-v /opt/processed:/opt/outgoing
```

Podman prepares those mount points inside the running container and mounts the host directories.

The image itself is **not modified**.

---

# 8. Build vs Run

## Build

```bash
podman build
```

Creates

```
Image
```

---

## Run

```bash
podman run
```

Creates

```
Container
```

These are completely different operations.

---

# 9. Images are Immutable Templates

Suppose:

```text
Image
```

creates

```text
Container A
Container B
Container C
```

Changing Container A does **not** affect:

* Container B
* Container C
* Image

Each container has its own writable layer.

---

# 10. Why "sleep infinity"?

Without:

```dockerfile
CMD ["sleep","infinity"]
```

Container:

```text
Starts
↓

Finishes
↓

Stops
```

With:

```dockerfile
CMD ["sleep","infinity"]
```

Container:

```text
Starts
↓

Keeps Running
↓

Can be Managed
```

Perfect for EX200 practice.

---

# 11. Rootless Container

A rootless container simply means:

The **container is owned by a regular user**, not root.

Example:

```
natasha
maria
walhalla
harry
```

The systemd service is stored in:

```
~/.config/systemd/user/
```

instead of

```
/etc/systemd/system/
```

---

# 12. EX200 Workflow

Almost every EX200 Podman question follows this pattern.

```text
Login as required user
        │
        ▼
podman run
        │
        ▼
podman generate systemd
        │
        ▼
Move service
(~/.config/systemd/user/)
        │
        ▼
systemctl --user daemon-reload
        │
        ▼
systemctl --user enable --now
        │
        ▼
loginctl enable-linger USER
        │
        ▼
Verify
```

Only these values change from question to question:

* Image Name
* Container Name
* Username
* Host Directory
* Container Directory
* Service Name

The workflow remains the same.

---

# 13. Relationship Between Components

```text
Containerfile
        │
        ▼
Image (Template)
        │
        ├────────► Container 1
        ├────────► Container 2
        ├────────► Container 3
        └────────► Container N
```

One image can create many containers.

One container cannot create an image unless explicitly committed.

---

# 14. Commands Worth Memorizing

## Build Image

```bash
podman build -t IMAGE_NAME .
```

or

```bash
podman build -t IMAGE_NAME -f MY_CONTAINERFILE .
```

---

## List Images

```bash
podman images
```

or

```bash
podman image list
```

---

## Run Container

```bash
podman run -d \
--name CONTAINER_NAME \
-v HOST_DIR:CONTAINER_DIR:Z \
IMAGE_NAME
```

---

## Generate systemd Service

```bash
podman generate systemd \
--name CONTAINER_NAME \
--files
```

---

## Reload systemd

```bash
systemctl --user daemon-reload
```

---

## Enable Service

```bash
systemctl --user enable --now container-CONTAINER_NAME.service
```

---

## Enable Linger

```bash
loginctl enable-linger USERNAME
```

---

# Final Takeaway

Instead of memorizing different EX200 questions, memorize **one workflow**.

```text
Containerfile
      │
      ▼
Build Image
      │
      ▼
Run Container
      │
      ▼
Generate systemd Service
      │
      ▼
Enable Service
      │
      ▼
Enable Linger
      │
      ▼
Verify
```

Once you understand this flow, most EX200 container questions become a matter of replacing:

* Image Name
* Container Name
* User Name
* Host Paths
* Container Paths
* Service Name

The underlying process stays the same.

